{{also|Fliegen}}
==German==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|vliegen}}, {{inh|de|goh|fliogan}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*fleugan}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*fleuganą}}, from {{der|de|ine-pro|*plewk-}}. Compare {{cog|nl|vliegen}}, Low German {{m|de|flegen}}, English {{m|en|fly}}, Danish {{m|da|flyve}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈfliːɡŋ̩/|/ˈfliːɡən/}}
* {{audio|de|De-fliegen2.ogg|audio (Germany)}}
* {{audio|de|De-fliegen.ogg|audio (Germany)}}
* {{audio|de|De-at-fliegen.ogg|audio (Austria)}}
* {{rhyme|de|iːɡn̩|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb|fliegen<flog,geflogen,flöge.haben,sein>}}

# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[fly]]; to [[travel]] by [[air]] {{+aux|de|sein}}
#* '''2010''', ''{{w|Der Spiegel}}'', issue [http://www.spiegel.de/spiegel/print/index-2010-52.html 52/2010], page 16:
#*: {{quote|de|Passagiere, die aus den USA nach Europa '''fliegen''' und dort umsteigen, sollen ab dem 1. April im Transitbereich nicht mehr kontrolliert werden.|It is planned that passengers who fly from the United States to Europe and change planes there are not checked in the transit area anymore after April 1.}}
# {{lb|de|intransitive|figuratively}} to [[rush]]; to [[fly]]; to go quickly {{+aux|de|sein}}
# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[fly]]; to [[pilot]] {{+aux|de|haben}}
# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[transport]] by air {{+aux|de|haben}}
# {{lb|de|intransitive|colloquial}} to [[get the axe]], to get [[kicked out]] {{+aux|de|sein}}
#: {{ux|de|... könnte von der Akademie '''fliegen'''.|... could '''get kicked out''' of academy.}}
# {{lb|de|intransitive|colloquial}} to [[fall]]; to fall down {{+aux|de|sein}}

====Conjugation====
{{de-conj|fliegen<flog,geflogen,flöge.haben,sein>}}
* In early modern German, the 2nd and 3rd persons singular present and the imperative singular were alternatively ''du'' {{m|de|fleugst}}, ''er'' {{m|de|fleugt}}, ''{{m|de|fleug}}!''

